Subject: text-input-v2: the leading-icon rule is two selectors, not an :is()

What: `[data-slot="text-input-v2-leading-icon"] :is(svg, [data-slot="icon-svg"])`
becomes the two selectors it stood for, one ending in `svg` and one in
`[data-slot="icon-svg"]`.
Why: Blink files a rule under its rightmost compound, and an `:is()` there goes
in the bucket every element is tested against. Ending in a tag and an
attribute, the rule is tested only against svgs and `data-slot` holders. Both
icon components put `data-slot="icon-svg"` on the svg itself, so an icon still
matches the attribute form, whose specificity equals the `:is()` form's, and
keeps the same computed style.

diff --git a/src/v2/components/text-input-v2.css b/src/v2/components/text-input-v2.css
index 1427846766..89ea59ef8c 100644
--- a/src/v2/components/text-input-v2.css
+++ b/src/v2/components/text-input-v2.css
@@ -73,7 +73,8 @@
   color: var(--v2-icon-icon-muted);
 }
 
-[data-component="text-input-v2"] [data-slot="text-input-v2-leading-icon"] :is(svg, [data-slot="icon-svg"]) {
+[data-component="text-input-v2"] [data-slot="text-input-v2-leading-icon"] svg,
+[data-component="text-input-v2"] [data-slot="text-input-v2-leading-icon"] [data-slot="icon-svg"] {
   display: block;
   flex: none;
 }
